1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,200
Hi! In this video we'll learn Gaussian elimination, a step-by-step method for solving systems of linear equations.

2
00:00:07,799 --> 00:00:18,333
Here are three equations in three unknowns, x, y and z. Can you see the solution just by looking? Not really: every equation mixes all three variables.

3
00:00:18,333 --> 00:00:27,766
Now look at this system. The last equation says seven z equals twenty-one, so z is three. Plug that in above, and y and x follow straight away.

4
00:00:27,766 --> 00:00:39,400
Here's the surprise: both systems have exactly the same solution. Gaussian elimination is the recipe that turns the hard system into the easy one. Let's see how, and why it works.

5
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:51,533
First, some notation. Only the numbers matter, not the letters. So we write the system as an augmented matrix: the coefficient matrix A, then a bar, then the right-hand-side column b.

6
00:00:51,533 --> 00:01:02,600
Each row is one equation: we'll call them R one, R two and R three. Each column belongs to one variable, x, y and z, and the last column holds the constants.

7
00:01:02,600 --> 00:01:15,233
We are allowed three elementary row operations. One: swap two rows. Two: multiply a row by a non-zero number k. Three: replace a row by itself plus c times another row.

8
00:01:15,233 --> 00:01:24,900
Our goal is row echelon form, a staircase shape. The first non-zero entry in each row is called a pivot, and every entry below a pivot is zero.

9
00:01:24,900 --> 00:01:36,033
By the end of this video, you'll be able to reduce a three by three system to echelon form, solve it by back-substitution, and explain why row operations never change the solution.

10
00:01:36,633 --> 00:01:46,566
Before calculating, let's picture it. An equation in three variables is a plane in space. Our three planes meet at a single point, and that point is the solution.

11
00:01:46,566 --> 00:01:53,500
Now apply our first row operation: R two becomes R two minus two R one. Watch the green plane.

12
00:01:53,500 --> 00:02:05,533
It tilts to a new position, but it still passes through the same point. Why? If a point satisfies both R one and R two, it automatically satisfies R two minus two R one.

13
00:02:05,533 --> 00:02:14,000
And the step can be undone: just add two R one back. So no solutions are gained, and none are lost. The solution set is preserved.

14
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:26,033
Next, R three minus R one, and then R three minus two R two. Look at the final plane: it is perfectly flat. Seven z equals twenty-one, which is the plane z equals three.

15
00:02:26,033 --> 00:02:32,833
Every step moved the planes, but never the meeting point. That is the whole idea behind Gaussian elimination.

16
00:02:33,633 --> 00:02:37,899
Now let's do the algebra carefully. Here is our augmented matrix.

17
00:02:37,899 --> 00:02:45,233
Step one. The first pivot is the one in the top-left corner. We use it to create zeros below it, in column one.

18
00:02:45,233 --> 00:02:57,766
To clear the two in row two, subtract two times row one. Two minus two is zero, one minus two is minus one, minus one minus two is minus three, and one minus twelve is minus eleven.

19
00:02:57,766 --> 00:03:00,299
The new row two replaces the old one.

20
00:03:00,299 --> 00:03:07,933
For row three, the leading entry is one, so we simply subtract row one. That gives zero, minus two, one, and minus one.

21
00:03:07,933 --> 00:03:13,933
Column one is now clear. Step two: our next pivot is minus one, in row two, column two.

22
00:03:13,933 --> 00:03:24,933
To eliminate the minus two below it, use R three minus two R two. Minus two plus two is zero, one plus six is seven, and minus one plus twenty-two is twenty-one.

23
00:03:24,933 --> 00:03:31,166
And there it is: row echelon form. Three pivots on a staircase, and zeros below every pivot.

24
00:03:31,166 --> 00:03:39,833
Now solve from the bottom up. This is called back-substitution. The last row says seven z equals twenty-one, so z equals three.

25
00:03:39,833 --> 00:03:50,833
Row two says minus y minus three z equals minus eleven. Substituting z equals three: minus y minus nine equals minus eleven, so y equals two.

26
00:03:50,833 --> 00:03:55,299
Row one: x plus two plus three equals six, so x equals one.

27
00:03:55,299 --> 00:04:05,933
Always check in the original equations. One plus two plus three is six. Two plus two minus three is one. One minus two plus six is five. All three check out.

28
00:04:06,533 --> 00:04:14,299
So what do these numbers mean? x equals one, y equals two, z equals three is the single point where all three planes meet.

29
00:04:14,299 --> 00:04:22,966
Echelon form also reveals the structure. Here every column of A has a pivot, so there are no free variables, and the solution is unique.

30
00:04:22,966 --> 00:04:38,899
But elimination can end differently. Ours ended with a pivot in every column. If instead a row becomes zero, zero, zero, bar five, it says zero equals five. That's impossible, so there is no solution: the planes share no common point.

31
00:04:38,899 --> 00:04:51,533
And if a row becomes all zeros, including the right-hand side, that equation added no new information. A variable is then free, and there are infinitely many solutions, like planes meeting along a line.

32
00:04:51,533 --> 00:04:57,466
So after elimination, the pivots tell you everything: one solution, none, or infinitely many.

33
00:04:58,066 --> 00:05:00,299
Let's recap three key takeaways.

34
00:05:00,299 --> 00:05:04,199
One: write the system as an augmented matrix, A bar b.

35
00:05:04,199 --> 00:05:10,966
Two: use row operations to reach echelon form. They change the equations, but never the solution set.

36
00:05:10,966 --> 00:05:20,499
Three: solve by back-substitution, check in the original equations, and read the pivots to know if the solution is unique, impossible, or infinite.

37
00:05:20,499 --> 00:05:26,699
Thanks for watching! References, tools and the AI disclosure are on screen now.
